## STRATEGIES & INSIGHTS

[sai-00001] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When a prompt requests an Excel or CSV output, prioritize the creation of the file structure (tabs, columns, formatting) over textual summaries. Treat the task as a data-processing operation rather than a text-generation task.
[sai-00005] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When a prompt includes a detailed rubric (e.g., bullet points within table cells, specific citation styles), treat these as hard constraints. Perform a post-generation verification step to ensure every cell and citation matches the requested format before finalizing the output.
[sai-00007] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: For complex technical deliverables, treat the prompt's rubric as a mandatory 'Definition of Done' checklist. Before drafting, generate a structural outline to ensure all required file formats (e.g., PDF, PNG diagrams), external links, and specific technical specifications are accounted for.
[sai-00009] helpful=3 harmful=0 :: When tasked with creating a foundational process blueprint or workflow document, perform a 'Requirement-to-Content' mapping: list every stakeholder, document type (e.g., TRSO, TRAR), and component (e.g., child parts) mentioned in the prompt. Before drafting, create a structural outline that explicitly allocates a section or bullet point to each of these identified items to ensure comprehensive coverage.
[sai-00011] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When a prompt requests the inclusion of instruments, tools, or appendices, do not merely list or name them. You must provide the actual structure, sample questions, or a detailed summary of the tool's content to demonstrate its applicability to the specific population and context requested.
[sai-00013] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When a prompt provides requirements that vary by subset (e.g., 'do X for items 1-5, do X+Y for items 6-10'), treat the most stringent requirements as the baseline for the entire set. Do not allow subset boundaries to limit the application of mandatory constraints like citations or specific question formats.
[sai-00016] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When a prompt includes a technical rubric or specific constraints (e.g., numeric TTLs, scalability strategies, data sync mechanisms), treat the response as a compliance exercise. Explicitly map every rubric item to a dedicated section or bullet point in your output to ensure no granular detail is omitted in favor of a high-level summary.
## FORMULAS & CALCULATIONS

[calc-00002] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: For variance analysis, always implement Excel formulas that handle potential division-by-zero errors (e.g., using IFERROR(formula, 0)) to ensure the output remains clean and functional.
